# Project Context Generator

Produces a single `PROJECT_CONTEXT.md` at the project root that serves as the canonical
reference for the entire codebase — accurate enough to onboard a new developer or give
an AI assistant complete context to work on any part of the project.

---

## Phase 1: Discover & Plan

Before reading any files, map the project so you know exactly what to read.

```bash
# Full directory tree (exclude node_modules, .git, dist, build, __pycache__, .venv)
find <path> \
  -not \( -path "*/node_modules/*" -o -path "*/.git/*" \
          -o -path "*/dist/*" -o -path "*/build/*" \
          -o -path "*/__pycache__/*" -o -path "*/.venv/*" \) \
  | sort
```

From the tree, identify:
- **Runtime**: Node/Python/Go/etc.
- **Framework hints**: `nest-cli.json` → NestJS; `manage.py` → Django; `go.mod` → Go; etc.
- **Frontend root**: `client/`, `frontend/`, `web/`, `src/` (look for `vite.config.*` or `next.config.*`)
- **Backend root**: `server/`, `api/`, `backend/`, or project root for monoliths
- **Key file lists** (build these before reading anything):
  - Manifests: `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`, `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml`
  - Entry points: `main.ts`, `main.py`, `cmd/main.go`, `index.ts`, `app.module.ts`
  - Entity/model files: `*.entity.ts`, `models.py`, `schema.prisma`, `*.model.ts`
  - Controller/route files: `*.controller.ts`, `routes/*.py`, `handlers/*.go`, `*.router.ts`
  - Frontend router: `App.tsx`, `router/index.ts`, `routes.tsx`, `_app.tsx`
  - Context/providers: `*Context.tsx`, `*Provider.tsx`, `store/`, `*.slice.ts`
  - API client: `api.ts`, `apiClient.ts`, `services/`, `hooks/use*.ts` (fetch hooks)
  - Config: `.env*`, `vite.config.*`, `tailwind.config.*`, `tsconfig.json`, `jest.config.*`
  - Docs/setup: `README.md`, `seed.ts`, `seed.py`, `docker-compose.yml`, `Dockerfile`, `.github/workflows/`

---

## Phase 2: Read Everything — No Skipping

Read **every file completely**. Do not summarize, infer, or guess. If a file is unreadable, note it explicitly.

### 2a. Manifests & Config
- Parse all dependency names **and versions** from `package.json` / `pyproject.toml` / `go.mod`
- Note `scripts` section from `package.json` (dev, build, test, seed commands)
- Read all `.env`, `.env.example`, `.env.development` files in full — capture every variable name and its purpose/default

### 2b. Entry Points & App Bootstrap
- Read `main.ts` / `main.py` / `app.module.ts` — note port, global middleware, CORS config, module imports

### 2c. Entities / Models / Schema
For **each** entity/model file, extract:
- Table/collection name
- Every field: exact name, type, nullable, default, unique, indexed
- Relations: type (OneToMany, ManyToOne, etc.), target entity, cascade rules
- Decorators / validators

For Prisma: read `schema.prisma` entirely and extract every model block.

### 2d. Controllers / Routes / Handlers
For **each** route file, extract:
- HTTP method (GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE)
- Exact path (including base prefix from module or router registration)
- Auth guard / role decorator (e.g., `@Roles('admin')`, `@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)`)
- Request body / query params shape (from DTO files if present — read those too)
- Brief description of what the handler does

### 2e. Frontend Router
- Read every route definition: path, component, lazy/eager, role/auth guard
- Note nested layouts or protected route wrappers

### 2f. Context / State / Store
- For each provider/context: what state it holds, what actions it exposes
- For Redux/Zustand slices: slice name, state shape, actions

### 2g. API Client / Service Layer
- Base URL config, how auth token is attached (headers, interceptors)
- List every API method: function name → HTTP method + path it calls

### 2h. Auth System
- Strategy: JWT / session / OAuth / API key
- Token storage: localStorage / cookie / memory
- How roles/permissions work

### 2i. README, Seeds, Deployment
- Read README fully — extract setup steps, env requirements, run commands
- Read seed scripts — what data they create
- Read `docker-compose.yml`, `Dockerfile`, CI workflows — extract deployment steps

